-- title:
--   Residual transcendence transfers between mutually integral elements
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative local ring, $K$ a field with an $A$-algebra structure, and $V \subseteq K$ a valuation subring, both $A$ and $V$ being local with their maximal ideals denoted $\mathfrak m$. Assume that the structure map $A \to K$ takes values in $V$ (hypothesis `hAV`), and that $V$ dominates $A$ in the sense that every $a \in \mathfrak m_A$ has image in $\mathfrak m_V$ when regarded, via `hAV`, as an element of $V$ (hypothesis `hmV`). Let $x, y \in K$ both lie in $V$, and assume $y$ is integral over the $A$-subalgebra $A[x]$ of $K$ generated by $x$, and symmetrically that $x$ is integral over $A[y]$. The conclusion is an equivalence between two conditions of the same shape, one for $x$ and one for $y$: for every monic polynomial $p \in A[X]$, whenever the value at $x$ of the coefficientwise image of $p$ in $K[X]$ lies in $V$ (this membership appearing as a hypothesis binder supplying the element of $V$), that element of $V$ is not in $\mathfrak m_V$; and likewise with $y$ in place of $x$.
--
--   In valuation-theoretic terms, the condition on $x$ says that the residue of $x$ in the residue field of $V$ admits no monic relation with coefficients from $A$, i.e. is transcendental over the residue field of $A$; the theorem states that this property passes between two elements of $V$ each integral over $A$ adjoined to the other. It is used in the analysis of Igusa-type valuation conditions on modular curves, where such residual-transcendence clauses must be moved between mutually integral coordinates.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ValuationSubring_forall_monic_aeval_not_mem_maximalIdeal_iff_of_isIntegral_adjoin.lean

